    "result_text": "All done. The full test suite passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`), including the target `coreFormatsTelephoneDisplay` acceptance test.\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe requirement: expose `telephoneDisplay` as the stored E.164 telephone formatted for humans (`+61412345678` \u2192 `+61 412 345 678`), while `telephone` stays raw E.164.\n\n1. **`OwnerTelephone.java`** (the existing telephone utility) \u2014 added a `toDisplay(String)` method alongside the existing `toE164(...)`. It reuses the class's existing country-code knowledge (`COUNTRY_CODES`, longest-match-first) to split the E.164 number into country code + national digits, then formats as `+<code> <national digits grouped in threes>`. Two small private helpers (`countryCodeOf`, `groupInThrees`) keep each piece focused. Returns `Optional.empty()` for anything that isn't a recognised E.164 number.\n\n2. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 added a `@Mapping(target = \"telephoneDisplay\", ...)` and a one-line `telephoneDisplay(owner)` default method delegating to `OwnerTelephone.toDisplay(...)`. This matches the pattern of the other derived fields (`displayName`, `initials`, etc.), so the formatting logic lives in the telephone utility, not in the mapper.\n\n3. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the read-only `telephoneDisplay` string property to the `Owner` schema (next to the other derived read-only fields), which regenerates `OwnerDto` with the new field. `telephone` remains an editable E.164 field, untouched.\n\nNo new dependencies were needed \u2014 the display formatting builds on the existing normalizer's country-code table.",
